@meta
id: 08-03
title: Overwrite a character in place
chapter: 08
type: lesson
skills: replace-r
par: 18
minutes: 3
vscode: double-click the value and retype it
open: order.repository.ts

@brief
Deleting a wrong character and typing the right one is two operations and
a trip through insert mode. When the replacement is exactly one character
long there is a key that does the whole thing without ever leaving normal
mode.

The page size and the offset in this repository were typed with the
letter `O` where a zero belongs. `take: 5O` is not a number.

Fix both. You should not see `-- INSERT --` at any point.

@keys
| `r` | replace the character under the cursor with the next one you type |
| `fO` | forward to the next capital `O` |
| `j` | down a line |

@hints
You are swapping one character for one character. That is a single
command, not a delete followed by an insert.
--
`r` waits for one more keypress and writes it over the character under
the cursor. It does not enter insert mode, so there is no `Esc`.
--
Get onto the `O` with `fO`, then `r0`. The next one is one line down.

@solution
    4j fO r0  j fO r0  :wq<Enter>

`r` is the answer to "that one character is wrong". It costs two
keystrokes, it leaves you in normal mode, and — the part that matters
later — it is one undo step and one repeatable command.

The rule of thumb for the next few lessons: one character for one
character is `r`. One character for several characters, or for nothing at
all, is a different key, and that is the next lesson.

`R` is its big sibling: it enters an overwrite mode where everything you
type replaces what is there until you press `Esc`. It is occasionally
right for ASCII tables and almost never right for code.

@start order.repository.ts
export class OrderRepository {
  public async findMany(customerId: string) {
    return this.prisma.order.findMany({
      where: { customerId },
      take: 5O,
      skip: 1O,
    });
  }
}

@expected order.repository.ts
export class OrderRepository {
  public async findMany(customerId: string) {
    return this.prisma.order.findMany({
      where: { customerId },
      take: 50,
      skip: 10,
    });
  }
}
